Security & Sharing Model Overview
Salesforce uses a layered approach to security—each layer narrows or expands access depending on business needs.

Salesforce’s security model is designed to protect your data and ensure that users can only access what they’re supposed to.

It works on multiple levels, from organization-wide settings down to individual field-level permissions, giving administrators granular control over who sees and does what in the system.

1. Organization Security
What is it?
Controls who can log in and when, regardless of what data they can see once in Salesforce.

Examples:
Restrict logins to trusted office IPs only.
Allow logins only during standard work hours (e.g., 8 AM–6 PM).
Real-Life Example:
Your HR team can log in from the corporate network but not from home to minimize data leak risk.
Scenario Question:
Q: How would you prevent users from accessing Salesforce from outside the office?
A: Implement IP range restrictions at the organization level.
2. Object-Level Security
What is it?
Controls access to all records of a given object (e.g., Accounts, Opportunities) by using profiles and permission sets.

Examples:
Only Sales users can create Opportunities.
Support users cannot delete Contacts.
Real-Life Example:
A Sales Rep’s profile grants “Read” and “Edit” access to Leads and Opportunities, while a Support Rep’s profile only lets them view those records.
Scenario Question:
Q: A new marketing user needs temporary access to the Product object. How would you provide this?
A: Assign a Permission Set with access to the Product object to that user.
3. Field-Level Security
What is it?
Restricts access to specific fields within an object.

Examples:
“Salary” field on Employee object visible to HR, hidden from others.
Marketing users cannot edit the “Discount Amount” field on Opportunities.
Real-Life Example:
The “Social Security Number” field is only visible to users with HR profiles; others can’t see or update this field.
Scenario Question:
Q: A user can access the Contact object but can’t see the “Personal Email” field. What’s the likely cause?
A: Field-level security settings for the user’s profile or permission set don’t allow access to that field.
4. Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD)
What is it?
Baseline access setting for records within an object for all users.

Examples:
Opportunities set to Private: users only see their own records.
Accounts set to Public Read Only: anyone can view, but only owners/editors can edit.
Real-Life Example:
Your sales reps shouldn’t see each other’s deals, so Opportunities OWD is set to Private. Only the owner and their superiors (via role hierarchy) have access.
Scenario Question:
Q: Why use OWD “Private” on Opportunities?
A: To ensure only the owner and selected users by hierarchy or sharing can access opportunity records.
5. Role Hierarchy
What is it?
A structure that allows higher roles to access records owned by subordinates.

Examples:
A Regional Sales Manager can see every deal owned by their team.
VP of Sales has visibility into all sales records under all regional managers.
Real-Life Example:
In a company, a Sales Director can see opportunities created by all sales reps reporting up through their chain.
Scenario Question:
Q: A manager needs to oversee all their team’s pipeline. What feature enables this?
A: Role Hierarchy grants upward visibility to managers.
6. Sharing Rules
What is it?
Automated rules that grant further access based on record ownership or field criteria.

Types:
Owner-based: Share all records owned by Sales East with the Marketing Group.
Criteria-based: Share all Opportunities over $50,000 with the Senior Management group.
Real-Life Example:
You have “Cases” set to Private OWD. A sharing rule shares all “Priority: High” cases with the Support Managers group.
Scenario Question:
Q: How do you allow all Marketing reps to see Accounts owned by the Sales team?
A: Create an owner-based sharing rule that shares Sales-owned Accounts with the Marketing role or group.
7. Manual Sharing
What is it?
Record owners or users with certain permissions can manually share individual records with other users or groups for ad-hoc needs.

Examples:
Alice (Account Owner) goes on leave; her manager shares the “MegaCorp” account with Bob so business continues.
Real-Life Example:
A recruiter is on vacation; she shares a candidate’s record with another recruiter manually for temporary access.
Scenario Question:
Q: When might you use manual sharing over sharing rules?
A: For one-off situations, like covering absences or special projects that aren’t addressed by existing rules.
8. Apex Managed Sharing
What is it?
Custom, programmatic record sharing by code when complex or dynamic sharing requirements can’t be handled by standard features.

Examples:
Records need to be shared based on logic from an external HR system.
Only share records with a custom “Collaboration Team” when a deal’s status changes.
Real-Life Example:
A “Project” record should be visible to a project team only if they are listed in a related “Team Members” object, managed dynamically using Apex triggers.
Scenario Question:
Q: When do you use Apex managed sharing?
A: When declarative sharing doesn’t fit—for example, sharing based on complex business logic or integration with an external system.
9. Public Groups
What is it?
Collections of users, roles, or other groups to be used in sharing rules or manual sharing.

Examples:
“Support Team” public group includes all support reps and managers.
Share all corporate event contacts with “Event Team” group.
Real-Life Example:
A Public Group called “Executives” includes CEO, CFO, and Legal, so high-value opportunities can be manually shared with this group at once.
Scenario Question:
Q: Why use Public Groups in sharing rules?
A: To simplify sharing configurations by referencing groups instead of individual users or roles.

Scenario Based Questions with Answers:

1. A user says they cannot see a sensitive field on the Account object. Where would you check and what would you update?
Steps to Resolve:
Review the user’s profile and permission sets for Field-Level Security on the Account object. Ensure the sensitive field is set to “Visible”.
Check if the field is also included in the page layout assigned to the user.
Confirm that no permission set or muting permission set is overriding access to that field.
If the field should not be hidden for that user, adjust permissions as needed to grant visibility.
2. You want all accounts owned by the “Sales West” team to be visible to the “Marketing” team. What kind of sharing would you use?
Best Approach:
Use an Owner-Based Sharing Rule.
How:
Create a sharing rule that shares Accounts where the owner is in the “Sales West” role (or public group) with the “Marketing” role or public group.
Set the access level (Read Only or Read/Write) as required.
3. A manager needs access to all the records owned by their subordinates, but a peer manager shouldn’t see their records. How do you set this up?
Solution:
Set the Role Hierarchy so that the manager’s role is above their team members’ roles.
Ensure the OWD for the object is not set to “Public Read/Write”—prefer “Private” or “Public Read Only” so only upward access through the hierarchy applies.
This setup grants managers access to records owned by their direct and indirect reports, but not to records owned by peer teams.
4. You’ve set Opportunity OWD to Private, but management needs reports showing all opportunities in the company. How will you solve this?
Steps:
Assign the management users a profile or permission set that includes the “View All” or “View All Data” permission for Opportunities.
Alternatively, position management roles above all sales users in the Role Hierarchy to grant them access to all subordinate Opportunity records.
Now management will have visibility for consolidated reporting.
5. A user on a temporary project needs edit access to specific records owned by another team member. What’s the quickest way to grant this?
Quickest Solution:
Use Manual Sharing.
How:
The record owner (or user with “Full Access”) opens the record, clicks “Sharing”, and shares the record with the specific user, granting edit rights.
6. Your company merges two divisions, and now users from both should see each other’s cases. Which feature will you use?
Approach:
Create a Criteria-Based Sharing Rule or Owner-Based Sharing Rule for the Case object.
How:
Create public groups for both divisions if not already present.
Set up a sharing rule to share Cases owned by users in one group/role with members of the other group/role, and vice versa.
