1. 🏢 Organization-Level Security

This controls high-level access for everyone in your org.

Example:

You can restrict login hours or IP ranges.
Prevent users from logging in unless they’re on company networks.
Settings include:

Login hours (Business Hours in Setup)
Trusted IP ranges (Network Access in Setup)
Password policies (Password policies in Setup) Like we have
User passwords expire in by default 90 days or we can set Never expires
Enforce password history (The default is 3 passwords remembered. You cannot select No passwords remembered unless you select Never expires for the User passwords expire in field.)
Session timeouts (Session Settings in Setup) :
We can set session timeout value like 2 hours
Force logout on session timeout
Lock sessions to the IP address from which they originated
Session Settings, Login IP Ranges, Login Hours and Password policies are present under profile also.


2. 🔐 User Authentication

Ensures users are who they say they are.

Example:

Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA): Users must enter a code sent to their phone after entering a password. (Identity Verification in Setup)
 enable MFA for all internal users in your org:

From Setup, in the Quick Find box, enter Identity, and then select Identity Verification.
Select Require multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all direct UI logins to your Salesforce org.
Single Sign-On (SSO): Log in once with corporate credentials. (Single Sign On Settings in Setup)
3. 🌐 Network Access Settings

Limits login to specific IP addresses. (Network Access in Setup)

Example:

Restrict logins only from office IPs (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24).
4. 📋 Profile Permissions

Profiles define what a user can do in Salesforce (object access, app access, system permissions).

Example:

A “Sales Rep” profile might allow read/create on Accounts but not delete.
An “Admin” profile has full access.
Key things Profiles control:

Object permissions (CRUD – Create, Read, Update, Delete)
Field permissions (but not granular field-level access — see #7)
App access
System permissions (like “Run Reports”)
Page Layout Assignments
Record Type Settings
Administrative permissions like Manage Leads, Apex Rest Services, Manage Users and all
Tab Permissions
Session Settings, Login IP Ranges, Login Hours and Password policies are present under profile also.
Enabled Apex Classes, VF Pages, Named Credentials, Custom Metadata and Custom Settings or any Custom Permission
5. 📊 Role Hierarchy

Determines data visibility based on hierarchy.

Example:

A Regional Manager sits above Team Leads in the role hierarchy.
The Regional Manager automatically sees all records owned by their subordinates.
Note: This only applies if OWD (see #8) is set to Public Read Only or Private.

6. 🔗 Sharing Rules

Automatically share records with certain groups of users when criteria are met.

Example:

Share all accounts in the “APAC” region with the APAC Sales team.
Share cases older than 3 days with Support Managers.
7. 🔒 Field-Level Security

Controls whether users can view or edit specific fields.

Example:

Hide the “Salary” field from non-HR users.
Allow Sales Reps to view but not edit “Credit Score”.
You can configure this via Profiles or Permission Sets .

8. 🗼 Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD)

Defines the baseline level of access for all users to each object.

Options:

Public Read/Write
Public Read Only
Private
Example:

Set Account OWD to Private : Only record owners and those above them in the role hierarchy can see the records.
Set Cases OWD to Public Read Only : Everyone can see all cases, but only owners can edit.
9. 🎯 Permission Sets & Permission Set Groups

Used to add extra permissions without changing a user’s profile.

Example:

A Sales Rep needs temporary access to edit Opportunities → assign a permission set granting Edit access.
Give a few users access to a new custom object without changing their profile.
🧩 Permission Set Group
A Permission Set Group, introduced in Winter ’21 , is a collection of permission sets . It allows you to bundle multiple permission sets together under one name and assign them collectively to users.
📌 A Permission Set Group is like a package that contains several permission sets — it simplifies management for complex roles.
✅ Key Differences Between Permission Set and Permission Set Group
Feature	Permission Set	Permission Set Group
Contains	Individual permissions	Multiple permission sets
Reusability	Can be reused across users	Combines reusable permission sets
Assignment	Assigned directly to a user	Assigned directly to a user
Conflict Handling	No conflict (single set)	Conflicts resolved by Salesforce (last assigned wins)
Use Case	For simple or focused access needs	For managing complex roles with many permissions
🚀 Real-Life Example: Managing Permissions in a Financial Services Company
Let’s say you work at a financial services company using Salesforce to manage clients, opportunities, and compliance data.
🧑‍💼 User Role: Compliance Officer
This role requires:
Read-only access to Accounts and Contacts
Full access to a custom object called Compliance_Review__c
Access to the Compliance app
Ability to run reports
A custom permission: Can_View_Sensitive_Data
Instead of creating one massive permission set , you could create smaller, reusable permission sets :
ReadOnly_Accounts
FullAccess_ComplianceReview
Compliance_App_Access
Report_Runners
CustomPerm_CanViewSensitiveData
Then, you create a Permission Set Group called Compliance_Officer_Role that includes all these permission sets.
Why this is better:
Each permission set can be reused for other roles (e.g., ReadOnly_Accounts might be used for interns too).
You can update individual permission sets without affecting others.
It’s easier to manage and understand than a huge single permission set.
🧠 Best Practices When Using Permission Set Groups
Use descriptive names so admins know what each group does.
Group related permissions logically (by job function, department, etc.).
Avoid overlapping groups to prevent confusion and conflicts.
Monitor effective permissions using the “Effective Permissions” feature in Setup.
🧪 Bonus Tip: How to Check Effective Permissions

Go to:
Setup > Permission Set Groups > [Select a group] > View Effective Permissions
Salesforce will show the combined permissions a user gets when the group is applied.
Summary
Concept	Description
Permission Set	A granular collection of permissions
Permission Set Group	A collection of permission sets for managing complex roles
Use Permission Sets for small, focused access needs.
Use Permission Set Groups when users need a combination of permissions from multiple areas of your org.
 
