Layer 3: Record-level security

Salesforce provides five ways to share records with others and access others’ records. 


In Salesforce, records have a field called “OwnerId” that points to a real user. Owners of records are usually people who created the record and have full CRUD access to it. 

Note: Ownership can also be granted to groups of users, for instance, queues. 

Types of record-level security (also known as record sharing rules)

1.  Record-level-security: organization-wide sharing defaults

Organization-wide defaults (OWD) control the default behaviour of how every record of a given object (for example, Accounts) is accessed by users who do not own the record.

For example:

If OWD for Accounts is Private, it means Maria can only see records she is a owner of.
If OWD for Accounts is Public Read Only, it means anyone can read (but not update or delete) the record.
If OWD for Accounts is Public Read/Write, it means anyone can read and update (but not delete) the record.
We can set OWD for objects under Sharing Settings in Setup


2 Record-level-security: role hierarchies

 An admin must first add the user to a role and grant access.


3. Record-level-security: sharing rules

Hierarchy-based sharing only works for sharing upward and in a vertical direction.
What if we want to share laterally?
For example, what if we want to share records that Maria owns with her peers in the service executive teams?
This is where sharing rules come in.
Sharing rules provides a way to share records laterally and in an ad-hoc fashion via public groups. Let’s investigate the details.
3.1 Ownership-based sharing rules

Ownership-based sharing rules let admins share records based on role, role-and-subordinate, and public group ownership.

For example, we can share all the records owned by anyone in a sales executive role (including Maria’s) with everyone in a service executive role. Similarly, we can share all the records owned by the sales executives, and their subordinates, with others as well.



Ah, got it — you’re asking whether you need to create separate sharing rules for each object (like Account, Contact, Opportunity, etc.) when you want to share records across different objects.

✅ Short Answer:

Yes, you must create separate sharing rules for each object in Salesforce.
Sharing rules are object-specific , meaning:

A sharing rule for Account does not affect Contact , Opportunity , or any other object.
You must define and configure sharing logic per object .
🔍 Why Sharing Rules Are Per Object

Salesforce enforces granular access control , so:

Each object has its own Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD) .
Each object can have its own set of sharing rules , manual sharing , and access settings .
This allows fine-grained control over who sees what on a per-object basis.
🧱 Example Scenario

Let’s say your goal is to give the “Sales Team” role access to certain records owned by the “Support Team”.

You would need:

Object	Sharing Rule Needed?	Purpose
Account	✅ Yes	Share Accounts owned by Support with Sales
Contact	✅ Yes	Share Contacts linked to those Accounts
Opportunity	✅ Yes	Share Opportunities owned by Support
Case	✅ Yes	Share Cases owned by Support
Each object needs its own ownership-based or criteria-based sharing rule .

💡 Tips When Managing Sharing Across Multiple Objects

Be Consistent
If the logic is similar (e.g., “Share all records owned by X with Y”), try to apply it consistently across relevant objects.
Use Public Groups or Roles
Define a shared group like “Sales Team”, and use that same group in sharing rules across multiple objects.
Automate Where Possible
Use Apex Managed Sharing or Flow/Process Builder if your sharing logic gets too complex across many objects.
Consider Record Relationships
For related objects like Contact (linked to Account), sometimes controlling access via the parent Account’s OWD/share settings is sufficient.
Review All Objects Individually
Don’t assume a rule on one object affects another. Always check and configure sharing for each object independently.
📌 Summary

Question	Answer
Do I need to create separate sharing rules for each object?	✅ Yes, sharing rules areobject-specific.
Can one sharing rule cover multiple objects?	❌ No, each object requires its own rule.
How do I manage this efficiently?	Use consistent roles/groups and automate where needed.
3.2 Criteria-based sharing rules

Criteria-based sharing rules let users access records based on the value of a field in a record, irrespective of who owns the record.
For example, if Maria wants to meet with all accounts in San Francisco, an admin might set a criteria-based sharing rule to share with Maria all accounts whose billing city is San Francisco.


3.3 Guest user sharing rules

A guest user sharing rule grants read-only record access to unauthenticated guest users. It is really a special type of criteria-based sharing rule.


3.4 Record-level-security: manual sharing

Manual sharing provides a mechanism to share individual records with others.
This permission is accessed through the Sharing button on the record details page, and lets end-users share individual record with others.
Note: This is only available if the OWD is private or public read-only because otherwise (say public read/write), you wouldn’t need it.

3.5 Record-level-security: Apex managed sharing

What is Apex Managed Sharing?

In Simple Words:

Apex managed sharing means writing custom code (in a language called Apex, which is like Java for Salesforce) to automatically give users access to specific records , when you can’t do it through the regular UI settings or sharing rules .

🧒 Analogy: Think of It Like a Custom Door Key

Imagine your Salesforce data as rooms (objects like Accounts, Contacts, etc.). Normally, you control who gets into each room using keys that are built-in (like roles, sharing rules, profiles).

But sometimes:

You need someone to get into a room only under special conditions .
Or maybe they should only get in if a certain field changes .
In those cases, you might need a custom key made just for that situation — and that’s where Apex managed sharing comes in.

💡 Real-Life Example (Maria’s Case)

Let’s go back to Maria:

Situation:

Maria notices that some premium accounts have a Customer Service Lead assigned via a custom field (Customer_Service_Lead__c).
She manually shares each premium account with that lead user — but it takes time and she forgets sometimes.
Problem:

The Customer Service Lead is not the owner of the record.
They only need access to some accounts — not all.
This can’t be handled by standard sharing rules because:
Sharing rules don’t support field-based sharing like this.
It depends on a custom field value changing.
Solution:

The admin team asks the developers to write an Apex trigger that does this:

Whenever a premium account has a Customer Service Lead assigned, automatically share that account with that user.

This is Apex managed sharing .

🛠️ How Does It Work?

Here’s what happens behind the scenes:

A new Account is created or updated.
The Customer_Service_Lead__c field is filled in.
The Apex trigger runs.
It creates a sharing record (e.g., AccountShare) that gives the specified user access to that Account.// Simplified version of what the code might look like
trigger SharePremiumAccounts on Account (after insert, after update) {

    List<AccountShare> accountShares = new List<AccountShare>();

    for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {

        if (acc.Type == ‘Premium’ && acc.Customer_Service_Lead__c != null) {

            accountShares.add(new AccountShare(

                AccountId = acc.Id,

                UserOrGroupId = acc.Customer_Service_Lead__c,

                AccessLevel = ‘Edit’

            ));

        }

    }

    insert accountShares;

}

✅ Why Can’t We Use Standard Sharing Rules Here?

Because:

Sharing rules can only use:
Record ownership
Field values from a limited set of fields (not all custom fields)
Criteria based on formulas or relationships that are supported
They can’t react dynamically to every change in a custom field.
They don’t run in real time like triggers do.
So, Apex managed sharing fills the gap when you need more flexibility than what the UI allows.

📌 When Do You Need Apex Managed Sharing?

You’ll probably need it if:

You want to share records based on custom logic .
You depend on field values that aren’t supported in sharing rules.
You want to automate complex sharing scenarios across multiple objects or users.
You need to cascade sharing between related records (e.g., share Opportunity when Account is shared).
🎯 Summary (Simple Version)

Concept	Explanation
Apex Managed Sharing	Writing code to automatically give users access to specific records.
Why needed?	Because standard tools (like sharing rules) can’t handle every possible scenario.
Example	Automatically sharing an Account with a “Customer Service Lead” when a field is filled.
How it works	Code runs when a record changes, creates a sharing entry (AccountShare,ContactShare, etc.)
Who uses it?	Admins + Developers for advanced security automation
What Is Apex Managed Sharing?

Apex managed sharing is a way to programmatically (via code) grant access to specific records for users or groups beyond the default sharing model (like OWDs, role hierarchies, or standard sharing rules).

It gives admins and developers fine-grained control over record-level access when standard tools aren’t enough .

🔑 Important: Only available in Enterprise Edition , Performance Edition , Unlimited Edition , and Developer Edition .

⚙️ How Does It Work?

Salesforce provides special objects called [Object]Share objects (e.g., AccountShare, ContactShare) that you can insert, update, or delete via Apex to control who gets access to which records.

Key Concepts:

You write an Apex trigger or class .
In the code, you create and insert a [Object]Share record.
This gives a user or group Read or Edit access to a specific record.
📌 Example Use Cases

Scenario	Why Apex Sharing Needed
Share premium accounts with assigned Customer Service Lead	Can’t be done via standard sharing rules if based on custom field
Share Opportunity with team members dynamically	Based on complex logic like territory, product type, etc.
Cascade sharing from Account to related Contacts	Standard parent-child sharing doesn’t support this by default
Grant temporary access for audit teams	Time-based or event-driven access
📦 Components Involved in Apex Managed Sharing

Let’s break down the main pieces you’ll work with:

1. [Object]Share Objects

These are the actual sharing records you manipulate via code.

Object	Share Object Name
Account	AccountShare
Contact	ContactShare
Opportunity	OpportunityShare
Custom Object (MyObject__c)	MyObject__Share
Each of these has fields like:

Field	Description
AccountId/ContactIdetc.	The ID of the record being shared
UserOrGroupId	The ID of the user or public group getting access
AccessLevel	‘Read’or‘Edit’
RowCause	Describes why the sharing exists (e.g., custom reason)
LastModifiedDate,LastModifiedById	Audit info
2. Custom RowCause (Optional but Recommended)

You should define a custom row cause using a custom metadata type or custom setting , so you can identify why a share was created.

Example:

apex

1

Schema.AccountShare.RowCause CUSTOM_CAUSE = Schema.AccountShare.RowCause.Custom_Territory_Share;

This helps avoid conflicts and lets you update/delete only your own shares later.

3. Public Groups or Roles

Sharing is usually done with:

A specific user
A public group
A role or role + subordinates
You store their IDs in the UserOrGroupId field.

4. Apex Trigger or Class

Most often, you’ll write a trigger on a record (like Account) that fires when something changes, and then create the appropriate share records.

🛠️ Step-by-Step: How to Create Apex Managed Sharing

✅ Step 1: Decide What You Want to Share

Example: Share Account records with a Customer Service Lead when they’re assigned.

✅ Step 2: Create a Custom Row Cause (Recommended)

Go to:

Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Row Causes
Click “New” and enter a label like “Customer_Service_Lead_Share”

Now you can refer to it in code:

apex

1

Schema.AccountShare.RowCause CUSTOM_CAUSE = Schema.AccountShare.RowCause.Customer_Service_Lead_Share;

✅ Step 3: Write Apex Trigger or Class

Here’s a simplified example:

trigger AutoSharePremiumAccounts on Account (after insert, after update) {

    List<AccountShare> accountShares = new List<AccountShare>();

    for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {

        Account oldAcc = Trigger.oldMap.get(acc.Id);

        // Only run if Customer_Service_Lead__c changed or is new

        if ((acc.Type == ‘Premium’) && acc.Customer_Service_Lead__c != null &&

            (Trigger.isInsert || (Trigger.isUpdate && acc.Customer_Service_Lead__c != oldAcc.Customer_Service_Lead__c))) {

            accountShares.add(new AccountShare(

                AccountId = acc.Id,

                UserOrGroupId = acc.Customer_Service_Lead__c,

                AccessLevel = ‘Edit’,

                RowCause = Schema.AccountShare.RowCause.Customer_Service_Lead_Share

            ));

        }

    }

    if (!accountShares.isEmpty()) {

        insert accountShares;

    }

}

✅ Step 4: Test Your Code

Use test classes to simulate different scenarios:

New premium account
Change in Customer Service Lead
Non-premium account (should not share)
Make sure you cover all cases and handle governor limits.

✅ Step 5: Deploy & Monitor

Once tested:

Deploy to production.
Monitor via:
Debug logs
Sharing button on records
Custom reports or dashboards
🧮 Governor Limits to Be Aware Of

Salesforce enforces strict limits on Apex sharing operations:

Limit	Description
150 DML statements per transaction	Each insert counts as one
10,000 DML rows per transaction	Total number of share records inserted
Query limits	Don’t forget SOQL query limits too
So always bulkify your code!

🔁 Updating or Removing Apex Shares

Sometimes you may want to:

Remove access when a field changes
Update access level
To do this:

Query existing shares with your custom row cause
Delete or modify them
Example:

List<AccountShare> existingShares = [

    SELECT Id

    FROM AccountShare

    WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND RowCause = ‘Customer_Service_Lead_Share’

];

delete existingShares;

🎯 Best Practices

Practice	Reason
Use a custom RowCause	Helps track why a share exists and avoid conflicts
Bulkify your code	Avoid hitting governor limits
Handle updates carefully	Only re-share when needed
Use triggers or scheduled jobs wisely	Ensure real-time or batch sharing works as expected
Document everything	Future admins will thank you
📋 Summary Table

Component	Purpose
[Object]Shareobject	Used to grant access to a record
UserOrGroupId	Who gets access (user/group/role)
AccessLevel	‘Read’or‘Edit’
RowCause	Explains why the share exists
Apex Trigger/Class	Automates the sharing logic
Public Group or Role	Defines who gets access
Governor Limits	Must follow Salesforce limits
💬 Final Thoughts

Apex managed sharing is powerful but should be used only when:

Standard sharing rules can’t do what you need.
You have dynamic or complex access requirements.
You need automation beyond manual sharing.
It’s not for beginners — but once you get the hang of it, it becomes a superpower for building secure, automated Salesforce systems.

